# Intelligence Hub — Phase B AI enhancement (optional, all fields optional)
#
# The site works fully without any of these set: every calculator's core
# result is a deterministic Layer 1 calculation with no network dependency.
# These only enable the optional "Get AI Insight" panel (Layer 2). If
# neither provider key is set, the panel degrades gracefully to an
# "AI insights are temporarily unavailable" message — never an error, never
# a blocked calculation.

# DeepSeek — tried first
DEEPSEEK_API_KEY=
DEEPSEEK_MODEL=deepseek-chat

# Gemini — fallback if DeepSeek is unset or its call fails
GEMINI_API_KEY=
GEMINI_MODEL=gemini-2.0-flash

# Kill switch — set to "false" to disable the AI Insight feature entirely
# (e.g. to stay inside the $20/month spend ceiling without removing keys).
# Any other value, or unset, leaves it enabled.
AI_FEATURES_ENABLED=true

# Intelligence Hub — admin dashboard (/admin), optional
#
# Without these set, /admin shows a plain "not configured" message and
# admits nobody — it's not reachable by default, the same fail-closed
# discipline the old password gate used. Two independent gates, both
# required:
#
# 1. Clerk auth — proves who's signed in. Create a free application at
#    https://dashboard.clerk.com, then copy its keys here.
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=
CLERK_SECRET_KEY=

# 2. The admin allowlist — a signed-in Clerk user still isn't
#    automatically trusted with lead/visitor data. Comma-separated list
#    of the exact email addresses (matched against the user's Clerk
#    account) allowed into /admin. Required even with Clerk configured —
#    an empty/unset value means nobody is admitted, not "anyone signed in".
ADMIN_ALLOWED_EMAILS=

# Visitor Intelligence 2.0 — Fingerprint device identification, optional.
#
# Without these, the site still tracks visitors/sessions/events (identify()
# falls back to the pre-existing localStorage-id path — see
# src/lib/visitorIntelligence.ts's identifyVisitor()), just without a
# real cross-browser-tamper-resistant visitor id, confidence score, or
# bot/VPN/incognito signals. Create a free trial at
# https://dashboard.fingerprint.com/signup, then copy these from
# Dashboard > API Keys (Public key) and the same page's Secret key.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FINGERPRINT_PUBLIC_API_KEY is safe to expose client-side —
# it's the JS agent's own init key, the same design as Firebase's client
# config below. FINGERPRINT_SERVER_API_KEY is a real secret: it's the
# only thing allowed to call Fingerprint's Server API and must never be
# NEXT_PUBLIC_-prefixed (see src/lib/fingerprintServer.ts).
NEXT_PUBLIC_FINGERPRINT_PUBLIC_API_KEY=
FINGERPRINT_SERVER_API_KEY=

# Region your Fingerprint workspace was provisioned in — "eu" or "ap".
# Leave unset for the default (Global/US) region. Set both of these to
# the SAME region (they're read by two different SDKs — the client
# @fingerprint/react agent and the server @fingerprint/node-sdk client).
NEXT_PUBLIC_FINGERPRINT_REGION=
FINGERPRINT_REGION=

# Firebase Admin SDK — privileged, server-only Firestore access for the
# admin dashboard's Overview, Leads, and Visitors tabs (bypasses
# firestore.rules entirely via a service account, unlike the public
# client writes below) — and for every /api/analytics/* route, which is
# how visitor/session/event writes reach Firestore at all now (see
# src/lib/visitorIntelligence.ts). Without these, /admin shows a clean
# "not configured" state instead of data, and visitor tracking silently
# no-ops (spec's "tracking failures must never break the site"). Generate
# a service account at
# Firebase Console > Project Settings > Service Accounts > Generate new
# private key, then copy the three fields from the downloaded JSON here
# (paste the private_key's PEM value as-is, including the literal \n
# sequences — the code converts them back to real newlines).
FIREBASE_ADMIN_PROJECT_ID=
FIREBASE_ADMIN_CLIENT_EMAIL=
FIREBASE_ADMIN_PRIVATE_KEY=

# Firebase client config — for lead capture (Contact/Demo forms, the
# gated /demo access flow) and the visitor-stats counter. These are NOT
# secrets (Firebase's own security model relies on firestore.rules, not
# hiding this config — see the comment in src/lib/firebase.ts), so
# src/lib/firebase.ts falls back to sane defaults if these are unset.
# Only set these to point the site at a *different* Firebase project
# than that default (e.g. a separate staging project).
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_API_KEY=
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN=
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_STORAGE_BUCKET=
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_MESSAGING_SENDER_ID=
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_APP_ID=

# Before lead capture or the admin dashboard's Firestore-backed tabs can
# work at all, the Firestore *database* itself needs to exist — Firebase
# Console > Build > Firestore Database > Create Database — and
# firestore.rules (repo root) needs to be deployed (paste into the
# Rules tab and Publish, or `firebase deploy --only firestore:rules`
# with the Firebase CLI). Neither is something this repo can do on its
# own; both require your own Firebase account access.

# EmailJS — the outbound half of the CRM's email channel (see
# docs/email-templates.md for the full setup + the actual template copy
# to paste into the EmailJS dashboard). The send button on a prospect's
# page (SendEmailPanel.tsx) calls POST /api/admin/crm/email/send, which
# sends server-side — via EmailJS's REST API directly, not their browser
# SDK — so the 20/day cap (enforced in that same route) is a real limit,
# not one a modified client could just skip. The four NEXT_PUBLIC_
# values are still safe to expose client-side (EmailJS's own security
# model is origin-restriction in your dashboard, not secrecy), but
# EMAILJS_PRIVATE_KEY below is a real secret — never NEXT_PUBLIC_-prefix
# it.
NEXT_PUBLIC_EMAILJS_PUBLIC_KEY=
NEXT_PUBLIC_EMAILJS_SERVICE_ID=
NEXT_PUBLIC_EMAILJS_TEMPLATE_COLD_OUTREACH=
NEXT_PUBLIC_EMAILJS_TEMPLATE_FOLLOWUP=

# EmailJS Private Key (Account > Security in the EmailJS dashboard) —
# required for the server-side send call above to bypass EmailJS's own
# origin/strict-mode check, which otherwise assumes every request comes
# from a browser. A real secret; without it, sending fails with
# "emailjs_not_configured".
EMAILJS_PRIVATE_KEY=

# Optional — how outbound emails are signed off. Sensible defaults
# ("Winston" / "DeployFleet") are used if these are unset; set
# EMAIL_REPLY_TO to a real inbox Winston actually checks, since the
# templates put it in the "reply-to" merge tag.
EMAIL_SENDER_NAME=
EMAIL_SENDER_ROLE=
EMAIL_REPLY_TO=

# Optional — persistent storage for the diesel-price admin edits.
# Without these, admin edits still work but only live in memory for the
# current server instance — gone on the next cold start/redeploy, which
# the /admin UI itself will tell you plainly. Attach any Redis/KV
# integration from the Vercel Marketplace (Upstash Redis is the current
# recommended one) and these get set automatically — no code change
# needed. UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL/UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN work the same
# way if you're wiring up your own Upstash instance directly.
KV_REST_API_URL=
KV_REST_API_TOKEN=
